WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:04.440
Feast your eyes upon these light&nbsp;
transport simulation algorithms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:10.040
ray tracers if you will. These enable us&nbsp;
to simulate the path of millions of light&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:15.560
rays bouncing in a scene and create&nbsp;
absolutely beautiful photorealistic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.560 --> 00:00:21.760
images. Hmm…but there is a huge&nbsp;
problem. What is the problem?

00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:26.200
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:32.200
Now, look at the previous technique.&nbsp;
Here is the problem. Noise. My goodness,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:36.960
so much noise, this would take quite&nbsp;
a bit of more time to get rid of,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:44.000
and now have a look at the new technique.&nbsp;
These images are so much cleaner.

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:50.320
So, are they usable? Well, we have noise&nbsp;
filtering algorithms tailored for light transport&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:56.720
simulations. Are they any good? Well, let’s&nbsp;
have a look together. Let’s plug in the previous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.720 --> 00:01:03.800
technique into a noise filtering algorithm.&nbsp;
And the input has these walking light blobs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.800 --> 00:01:11.760
so…are they gone now? Unfortunately not.&nbsp;
This is supposed to remove noise, not fix&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:19.480
these blobs. So let’s see what happens with the&nbsp;
new technique…and…oh my. I already see that we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.480 --> 00:01:28.360
have less noise, and after filtering…bingo!&nbsp;
Love it. Not perfect, but so much better.

00:01:28.360 --> 00:01:33.920
So, let’s have a closer look at what just&nbsp;
happened. Here is a comparison with path&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:40.520
tracing. This is one of the first algorithms&nbsp;
you learn on your light transport class. It can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.520 --> 00:01:47.520
barely do anything on this notoriously difficult&nbsp;
scene. MMIS, this is marginal multiple importance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:54.800
sampling, now this is so much better. However&nbsp;
with this, we also get lots of noise. This is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.800 --> 00:02:02.240
the ReSTIR technique that you just saw with the&nbsp;
blobs. This is from 2022 and gives us less noise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:07.680
that is good, however, look. The specular&nbsp;
highlights on the teapot are different,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:14.800
they do not match the true simulation. It does&nbsp;
not give us the image we are looking for. And now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:22.440
look at the new technique, oh yes, less noise&nbsp;
and it is correct at the same time. This was a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:28.080
particularly good case for the new technique,&nbsp;
but even on a selection of other scenes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:34.680
it performs really well. Better than its&nbsp;
predecessors. A solid step forward. Loving it!

00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:39.160
And we are not done yet, not even close. I have a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.160 --> 00:02:44.040
bunch of incredible new papers on&nbsp;
ray tracing for you to marvel at!

00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:49.920
When creating games and virtual worlds,&nbsp;
we often find ourselves having 3D geometry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:56.040
that is not too detailed, but we would like to&nbsp;
make it look a bit more interesting. Slapping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.040 --> 00:03:02.760
a texture on it kinda works, but it is&nbsp;
still very flat and not very exciting.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.760 --> 00:03:09.000
Now we would like this geometry to stay&nbsp;
simple and quick to render, easy to store,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:16.040
but at the same time, to appear to be a lot&nbsp;
more detailed. Look. So much better! This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.040 --> 00:03:21.280
technique is called displacement mapping&nbsp;
and it can make a really simple piece of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:29.760
geometry come alive in your games and virtual&nbsp;
worlds. However, it is not new. Not new at all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:37.280
it goes back 30 years, possibly even longer.&nbsp;
So, what is interesting here in this new paper?

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:38.640
Well, two things.

00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:45.440
One, hold on to your papers Fellow Scholars&nbsp;
because it can do all this up to 11 times&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.440 --> 00:03:51.760
faster than previous techniques, now&nbsp;
is the point where I immediately think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.760 --> 00:03:59.320
that potentially by leaning more heavily on&nbsp;
memory, so maybe faster at the cost of using&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.320 --> 00:04:06.600
more memory. However, I was very surprised to&nbsp;
find out that this was not only not the case,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.600 --> 00:04:12.560
but it also requires 3 times&nbsp;
less memory. That is incredible.

00:04:12.560 --> 00:04:19.760
Two, it takes a possibly fractional level of&nbsp;
detail parameter. That is very cool. You see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.760 --> 00:04:26.080
level of detail helps you save resources by&nbsp;
rendering a coarse version of a piece of geometry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.080 --> 00:04:33.080
and add details to it only as we get closer. This&nbsp;
is when we would see them, after all. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.080 --> 00:04:41.520
this often happens in steps, where you can often&nbsp;
see from detail level 0 to 1 or 1 to 2, this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.520 --> 00:04:49.400
object suddenly looks like this object. There is&nbsp;
a pop. However, look. With a fractional level of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.400 --> 00:04:56.600
detail parameter, we can make it pop a little less&nbsp;
and these transitions will look more continuous.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.600 --> 00:05:03.720
Note that other techniques can do this too, but&nbsp;
this is one more tool in our toolkit. So cool!

00:05:03.720 --> 00:05:08.120
I also could not resist showing&nbsp;
you this real-time technique that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.120 --> 00:05:13.480
can simulate a bit of light transport, for&nbsp;
instance, it can do indirect illumination,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.480 --> 00:05:19.640
or color bleeding. This is the bleeding effect&nbsp;
of light as it bounces through multiple diffuse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.640 --> 00:05:27.760
surfaces and is a beautiful phenomenon in nature,&nbsp;
but unfortunately, less so in many virtual worlds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.760 --> 00:05:33.880
because it is so slow and expensive to&nbsp;
compute. But not here. This technique&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.880 --> 00:05:39.640
can pull off limited light transport but&nbsp;
in real time. And the implementation of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.640 --> 00:05:47.360
it is available on Shadertoy where you can try it&nbsp;
right now. And even look at the code or modify it.

00:05:47.360 --> 00:05:54.840
And we are still not done yet. This new technique&nbsp;
can create this gorgeous material by shooting more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.840 --> 00:06:03.880
than 2 million rays at it per second, and it uses&nbsp;
less than 200 Megabytes of memory. That is almost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.880 --> 00:06:11.040
nothing for a geometry of this detail. This is a&nbsp;
SIGGRAPH ASIA-paper, which is perhaps the olympic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.040 --> 00:06:18.240
gold medal of computer graphics research,&nbsp;
written by a single author. Bravo Shinji!

